PROCESS- A process is a running instance of a program. It has its own process ID (PID), uses system resources like CPU and memory,
  and can create child processes when it starts other programs.

PS (Process Status)
Basic Syntax: ps [options]
Flag	             Description
-e or -A	   Show all processes
-f	         Full-format listing
-l	         Long format
-u [user] 	Show processes for specific user #ps -u john -o pid,ppid,cmd,time
-p [PID]	  Show specific process by PID
-o	        Custom output format # -o in ps means format output: it lets you choose exactly which columns to display instead of using the default process listing
--sort	    Sort by specific column
-C [cmd]	  Select by command name
aux	       BSD-style: all processes, all users, with extra info

regex example:
ps -eo pid,ppid,cmd,%mem,%cpu --sort=-%mem #custom format: PID, Parent PID, Command, Memory%, CPU%, sorted by memory descending #--sort=[option]
ps -C nginx -o pid,user,cmd,%cpu,%mem #Shows all nginx processes with their resource usage
  # -C nginx: filter to processes whose command name is nginx.
. #-o pid,user,cmd,%cpu,%mem tells ps to print only those fields, in that order

WATCH runs a command repeatedly at a fixed interval and refreshes the terminal display so you can see changes over time.
By default, watch reruns the command every 2 seconds and keeps going until you stop it with Ctrl+C.
It is useful for monitoring disk usage, process counts, log files, and service status without having to type the same command repeatedly.
Basic syntax: watch [options] command
FLAGS
-n seconds: changes the refresh interval from the default 2 seconds.
-d: highlights changes between updates.
-t: hides the header line with the time and command.
-g: exits when the output changes.

Practical examples
watch df -h: keeps an eye on disk space.
watch free -m: monitors memory usage.
watch "ps aux | grep nginx": repeatedly checks for nginx-related processes. Quoting is important when using pipes or multiple  shell operators
watch 'ps -C postgres -o pid,user,time,%cpu,%mem --no-headers' #monitoring specific service

TOP (Table of Processes)- displays overall system stats such as uptime, load average, task counts, CPU usage, memory, and swap usage. 
Below that, it lists individual processes with columns like PID, user, CPU, memory, running time, and command.
Basic Syntax: top [options]
Flag            	Description
-u [user]   	Show only specific user's processes #top -u nginx -  User-Specific Monitoring
-p [PID]     	Monitor specific PIDs only #top -p 1234,5678,9012 - Monitor Multiple PIDs
-n [count]  	Number of iterations before exiting #top -b -n 1 -o %MEM | head -20 - Batch Mode for Scripting
-d [seconds]	 Delay between refreshes
-b	           Batch mode (non-interactive) #method of processing data where a program executes a series of commands or processes without user interaction.
-H	           Show threads
-i	           Ignore idle processes

Interactive Commands (Inside top):
Key	         Action
P     	Sort by CPU usage (default)
M     	Sort by Memory usage
T     	Sort by Time/Running time
N	      Sort by PID
k	      Kill a process (enter PID)
r	      Renice a process #changes the priority of a process that's already running — it doesn't start a new one
f     	Select which columns to display
o	      Change sort field
u	      Filter by user
H	      Toggle thread view
e	      Toggle memory units
q	      Quit
1	      Show per-CPU usage (for multi-core)

HTOP (Interactive Process Viewer) - is an interactive, real-time process viewer — basically a nicer, more usable version of top.
Basic Syntax: htop [options]
Flag	               Description
-u [user]	      Show only specific user  #user htop -u mysql - user isolation
-p [PID]	      Show only specific PID
-t	            Tree view mode
-s [COLUMN]	    Sort by specific column #htop -s PERCENT_MEM - sorts by memory percentage instead of the default
-d [seconds]  	Delay between updates
-C	            Use monochrome colors
--pid [PID]	    Same as -p

Interactive Commands (Inside htop):
Key	            Action
F1 or h        	Help
F2 or S     Setup/Menu
F3 or /	     Search
F4 or \    	Filter processes
F5 or t	   Tree view toggle
F6	     Sort by selected column
F7 or ]	 Increase niceness (lower priority)
F8 or [	 Decrease niceness (higher priority)
F9 or k	  Kill process
F10 or q   	  Quit
Space     	Tag process
U	      Show processes of current user only
u	      Show processes of specific
P      	Sort by CPU
M     	Sort by Memory
T     	Sort by Time
I     	Invert sort order
H     	Show/Hide user threads
K     	Show/Hide kernel threads
F	      Follow process
e	      Show environment variables

Setup Menu (F2) Features:
* Columns: Choose displayed columns (ST_UID, PID, PPID, CPU%, MEM%, etc.)
* Colors: Customize color scheme
* Meters: Add/remove CPU/Memory/Swap meters
* Display options: Toggle tree view, hide threads, etc.

