CHOOSE THE RIGHT TOOL:
Use ps when:
- Writing scripts and automation
- Need to capture a single snapshot
- Processing output with grep/awk/sed
- Remote monitoring via SSH (minimal overhead)

Use top when:
- Quick system health check
- Limited system resources (minimal overhead)
- Standard across all Linux distros (always available)
- Need simple real-time monitoring

Use htop when:
- Deep investigation and troubleshooting
- Need tree view for process hierarchy
- Want better visual interface
- Need to kill/reprioritize processes interactively
- Working on development/debugging
- Have it installed (not always default)

KILL -kill sends a signal to a process (usually to terminate it), targeting it by PID.

Signal Number    	Signal Name          	Description
1	                 SIGHUP       	Hangup - often used to reload configs
2	                 SIGINT	        Interrupt - same as Ctrl+C
9	                 SIGKILL	      Kill - forceful, immediate termination (can't be caught/ignored)
15               	 SIGTERM	      Terminate - graceful shutdown (default)
18	               SIGCONT	      Continue - resume stopped process
19	               SIGSTOP	      Stop - pause process (can't be caught/ignored)
20	               SIGTSTP	      Terminal stop - same as Ctrl+Z
Common Flags:
Flag          	Description
-l	          List all signal names #kill -KILL 1234
-s [signal] 	Specify signal by name  #kill -TERM 1234  #kill -KILL 1234 #kill -HUP 1234 #kill -s USR1 1234
-[number]    	Specify signal by number
-n [signal]  	Specify signal number #kill -15 1234
--	          Indicates end of options (use with negative PIDs)

KILLALL - killall sends a signal to processes by name instead of PID — kills all matching processes at once.
Basic Syntax: killall [options] <process_name> 
# killall firefox        # sends SIGTERM to all processes named "firefox"
# killall -9 firefox      # uses the same signal and names with KILL
Flag	Description
-s [signal]	Signal to send (default SIGTERM)
-q	Quiet mode (no error messages)
-i	Interactive - confirm before killing #killall -i python3 # Will ask: "Kill python3(1234)? (y/N)" for each process
-v	Verbose - report what was done
-w	Wait for processes to die # killall -w mysqld # Waits until mysqld actually exits
-l	List all signal names
-u [user]	Kill only processes owned by specific user # killall -u john chrome # Kills only John's Chrome processes
-g	Kill process group instead of process
-r	Interpret process name as regex # killall -r ".*python.*" # Kills all processes with "python" in their name
-o [time]	Kill only processes older than
-y [time]	Kill only processes younger than
-I	Ignore case in process name

3. PKILL Command - kills processes by matching a pattern against the process name or command line — more flexible than killall.
Key difference from killall: pkill does partial/regex matching by default, so pkill fire would also match firefox.
NOTE: Be careful — a loose pattern can kill more than you intend.
Basic Syntax: pkill [options] <pattern>
Flag          	Description
-f          	Match full command line (not just name)
-x    	      Match exact process name
-u [user]   	Match processes owned by user #pkill -u john chrome # Kills John's Chrome processes
-U [user]	    Match processes by real user ID
-g [PGID]	    Match processes in process group
-P [PPID]	    Match child processes of parent PID #pkill -P 1234 # Kills all children of process 1234
-t [terminal]	Match processes by terminal
-l	          List process name along with PID #pkill -l firefox # Shows: "1234 firefox"
-n	          Only newest process
-o	          Only oldest process #pkill -o python3 # Kills the oldest Python process
-f          	Match against full command line #pkill -f "python3 /home/user/script.py" # Kills only the specific script, not all Python processes
-signal	      Signal to send
-c	          Count matching processes #pkill -c chrome # Returns number of Chrome processes (without killing)
-v	          Invert match
 
Signal Options:
Flag	          Description
-signal	      Send signal (e.g., -9, -TERM)
--signal      signal	Alternative syntax
-e	          Echo killed PIDs

COMPARISON TABLE:
Feature             	    KILL	              KILLALL       	PKILL
Target	Specific          PID	             Process name	      Pattern/Regex
Multiple Processes	No (one at a time)  	Yes (all matching)	Yes (all matching)
Pattern Matching        	No	             Yes (limited)	    Yes (regex, full cmd)
User Filtering	          No	              Yes (-u)	        Yes (-u, -U)
Age Filtering           	No	              Yes (-o, -y)	    Yes (-o, -n)
Interactive Mode	        No              	Yes (-i)         	No
Wait for Exit	            No	              Yes (-w)        	No
Kill by Parent	    Via process group	       No	              Yes (-P)
Terminal Filter         	No	               No             	Yes (-t)
Signal Default	       SIGTERM (15)	      SIGTERM (15)    	SIGTERM (15)
Common Use	          Single process    	All instances	   Pattern-based
Safety	              Must know PID     	Can be dangerous	Most dangerous


PROCESS SELECTION FLOWCHART:
text
Need to terminate a process?
│
├── Do you know the exact PID?
│   └── YES → Use KILL
│       ├── Normal shutdown → kill <PID>
│       ├── Force kill → kill -9 <PID>
│       └── Reload config → kill -HUP <PID>
│
├── Do you know the exact process name?
│   └── YES → Use KILLALL
│       ├── All instances → killall firefox
│       ├── Interactive → killall -i firefox
│       └── User-specific → killall -u john firefox
│
└── Do you need pattern matching or complex criteria?
    └── YES → Use PKILL
        ├── Full command → pkill -f "python.*main.py"
        ├── By user → pkill -u guest
        ├── Parent child → pkill -P 1234
        └── Terminal → pkill -t pts/2
